{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the sum of all pairwise differences of outcomes between the two lotteries. In this case, the outcomes are: Lottery A offers 5000 Euros with 0.1% probability and 0 Euros with 99.9% probability; Lottery B offers 5 Euros with 100% probability. The pairwise difference in outcomes is |5000 - 5| = 4995. Since there is only one non-zero outcome in A and one certain outcome in B, the only relevant pairwise difference is between the 5000 Euro outcome of A and the 5 Euro outcome of B. The sum of all pairwise differences is therefore 4995 (only one pair exists, as the 0 Euro outcome in A and the 5 Euro outcome in B differ by 5, but the reason considers all pairwise differences — however, the problem does not specify whether it is across all outcome pairs or just between the non-zero outcomes). But note: the reason says 'sum of all pairwise differences of outcomes between the lotteries'. This implies considering all outcome values and computing differences across the lotteries. So, we have: A has outcomes 5000 (0.1%) and 0 (99.9%), B has outcome 5 (100%). The pairwise differences are: |5000 - 5| = 4995 and |0 - 5| = 5. The sum of all pairwise differences is 4995 + 5 = 5000. This sum is higher for A than for B (if we were to compute for B, it would have only one outcome, so no pairwise differences with other outcomes). But the reason is about differences between the lotteries — so the sum of absolute differences between each outcome of A and each outcome of B. Thus, the total sum is |5000 - 5| + |0 - 5| = 4995 + 5 = 5000. Therefore, the sum of outcome differences is 5000 for A (since it has two outcomes), and for B, it would be only one outcome, so no pairwise differences with A — but the reason compares the sum of pairwise differences between the lotteries. So the sum is 5000 for A. For B, there is only one outcome, so the pairwise differences with A would be |5 - 5000| and |5 - 0|, same as above — so the sum is the same: 5000. Thus, both lotteries have the same sum of pairwise differences: 5000. Therefore, the reason would be indifferent between A and B. However, the reason says it prefers the lottery with the more favorable sum — if equal, it may be indifferent. So the decision reason is applicable and can be applied to this decision problem.",
